 0 & 1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & 2 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 4 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that a matrix of rank $6$ must have at least $6$ columns and at least $6$ rows. \nSo, we disregard the matrix $A$ since it has only $5$ columns and the matrix $B$ since it has only $4$ columns.\n\nAlso, the matrix must have $6$ pivot columns. As we can see, the matrix $C$ has exactly $6$ pivot columns. So, $\\text{rank}(C)=6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425057 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65028e55-3a8b-521e-8771-7d7fd0667387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425023} {:answer/id #uuid "66b70f53-aa24-525d-8ce3-88738f313f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425024} {:answer/id #uuid "a818e102-2e5b-547e-9d5e-908647988fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425033} {:answer/id #uuid "aa1f3aad-6a31-57d1-aef1-e79d1174936a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425034} {:answer/id #uuid "d6825204-482f-5521-8bda-c88be8485051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65028e55-3a8b-521e-8771-7d7fd0667387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425023} :answer-field/id #uuid "875bc03a-5e72-5af8-9376-22adf38e16a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186425052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e4ed9e5-eccc-52bc-8945-b31db9b251e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-35285" :question/problem "If \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]$, then $\\text{rank}(A)=$" :question/worked-solution "To find the rank of $A,$ we first reduce it to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -6 & -9 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix has $2$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $2$ non-zero rows).\n\nTherefore, $\\text{rank}(A)=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321049 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f5a44c9-16e5-55fb-a10f-15435a0a9a4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Rank of a Matrix Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccfd5f3-f6e4-5427-8e12-af3c11112280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319426} {:answer/id #uuid "71551415-75d4-548b-bca9-d1522d903948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/i